1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,707
December 2013. New York.

2
00:00:01,707 --> 00:00:06,759
A red carpet, flashbulbs, and one of the
year's biggest film premieres.

3
00:00:06,759 --> 00:00:12,592
The movie is The Wolf of Wall Street. A
story about a stockbroker who gets rich by

4
00:00:12,592 --> 00:00:13,944
lying to investors.

5
00:00:14,944 --> 00:00:19,674
According to the United States Justice
Department, the money that made this film

6
00:00:19,674 --> 00:00:21,980
had been misappropriated from Malaysia.

7
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:24,168
Not from Wall Street. From a country.

8
00:00:24,718 --> 00:00:29,769
The same money, prosecutors say, bought a
superyacht, mansions in Beverly Hills and

9
00:00:29,769 --> 00:00:33,421
New York, a private jet, and paintings by
Monet and Picasso.

10
00:00:33,421 --> 00:00:38,107
It came from a fund that was supposed to
belong to every citizen of Malaysia.

11
00:00:38,357 --> 00:00:42,960
And twelve years later, the man who ran
the country at the time has been

12
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:43,600
convicted.

13
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:48,843
This is the story of 1MDB. How the money
left, who followed it, and why Malaysians

14
00:00:48,843 --> 00:00:50,377
are still paying for it.

15
00:00:51,877 --> 00:00:56,162
In April 2009, Najib Razak became the
prime minister of Malaysia.

16
00:00:56,162 --> 00:01:01,568
He was political royalty. His father had
been the country's second prime minister.

17
00:01:01,568 --> 00:01:02,953
His uncle, the third.

18
00:01:02,953 --> 00:01:05,656
He also kept his job as finance minister.

19
00:01:06,206 --> 00:01:11,911
That same year, his government took over a
fund set up by the state of Terengganu and

20
00:01:11,911 --> 00:01:15,669
renamed it 1Malaysia Development Berhad.
1MDB for short.

21
00:01:15,919 --> 00:01:21,632
The idea sounded modern and ambitious.
Borrow money, invest in energy and real

22
00:01:21,632 --> 00:01:25,660
estate, and build a financial district in
Kuala Lumpur.

23
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:30,641
Najib chaired the fund's board of
advisers. Big decisions needed his

24
00:01:30,641 --> 00:01:31,300
approval.

25
00:01:31,850 --> 00:01:36,959
There was another figure close to the
fund. A young Malaysian financier named

26
00:01:36,959 --> 00:01:39,016
Low Taek Jho, known as Jho Low.

27
00:01:39,016 --> 00:01:44,390
He was not yet thirty, a graduate of the
Wharton business school in Pennsylvania,

28
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:48,437
and famous in Kuala Lumpur for knowing
everyone who mattered.

29
00:01:48,687 --> 00:01:50,812
He held no official job at 1MDB.

30
00:01:50,812 --> 00:01:55,262
But according to the US Justice
Department, he helped set it up and

31
00:01:55,262 --> 00:01:57,985
steered its money from behind the scenes.

32
00:01:57,985 --> 00:02:03,364
Jho Low has been charged in the United
States and in Malaysia. He has never stood

33
00:02:03,364 --> 00:02:05,755
trial, and he denies any wrongdoing.

34
00:02:06,955 --> 00:02:13,725
The first big deal came in September 2009,
just months after the fund was born.

35
00:02:13,725 --> 00:02:20,238
1MDB announced a joint venture with
PetroSaudi, a small private oil company.

36
00:02:20,238 --> 00:02:22,980
1MDB put in one billion dollars.

37
00:02:23,530 --> 00:02:29,181
According to the US Justice Department, a
large part of that money never reached the

38
00:02:29,181 --> 00:02:29,720
venture.

39
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:35,035
Instead, prosecutors say, more than one
billion dollars over the next two years

40
00:02:35,035 --> 00:02:39,946
was sent to a Swiss bank account belonging
to a company called Good Star.

41
00:02:40,196 --> 00:02:43,495
And Good Star, they say, was controlled by
Jho Low.

42
00:02:44,495 --> 00:02:49,750
Prosecutors say the fund's board was
misled about where the money was going.

43
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:55,075
On paper, 1MDB still owned something
valuable. The real cash, according to US

44
00:02:55,075 --> 00:02:57,149
prosecutors, was already gone.

45
00:02:57,699 --> 00:03:03,148
Years later, in August 2024, a Swiss
federal court convicted two PetroSaudi

46
00:03:03,148 --> 00:03:08,815
executives of fraud and money laundering
over this deal, and sentenced them to

47
00:03:08,815 --> 00:03:10,268
seven and six years.

48
00:03:10,268 --> 00:03:14,046
Both men appealed. PetroSaudi has denied
wrongdoing.

49
00:03:14,296 --> 00:03:19,239
But in 2009, nobody outside a small circle
knew any of this.

50
00:03:19,239 --> 00:03:22,781
And the fund was about to borrow much
more.

51
00:03:23,981 --> 00:03:30,508
In 2012 and 2013, 1MDB raised six and a
half billion dollars by selling bonds.

52
00:03:30,508 --> 00:03:35,277
The bank that arranged all three deals was
Goldman Sachs.

53
00:03:35,527 --> 00:03:40,929
For that work, Goldman earned roughly six
hundred million dollars in fees. Far more

54
00:03:40,929 --> 00:03:43,793
than banks usually make on deals like
these.

55
00:03:44,343 --> 00:03:49,945
Here is how, according to the US Justice
Department, the money was moved.

56
00:03:49,945 --> 00:03:55,471
The 2012 bonds were backed by a real Abu
Dhabi state fund, IPIC, and its

57
00:03:55,471 --> 00:03:56,852
subsidiary, Aabar.

58
00:03:57,102 --> 00:04:01,971
But over one billion dollars was then
wired to a different company, set up in

59
00:04:01,971 --> 00:04:05,385
the British Virgin Islands, with almost
the same name.

60
00:04:05,385 --> 00:04:09,369
A lookalike. Prosecutors say it was not
part of the real Aabar.

61
00:04:09,919 --> 00:04:15,731
In 2013, the pattern repeated. Out of a
three-billion-dollar bond, about 1.26

62
00:04:15,731 --> 00:04:21,996
billion dollars, prosecutors say, went to
an account in Singapore held by a company

63
00:04:21,996 --> 00:04:23,053
called Tanore.

64
00:04:23,603 --> 00:04:27,252
And then came the most dangerous transfer
of all.

65
00:04:27,252 --> 00:04:33,506
In March 2013, 681 million dollars landed
in a private bank account in Kuala Lumpur.

66
00:04:33,506 --> 00:04:38,495
The US complaints called the account
holder "Malaysian Official 1".

67
00:04:38,745 --> 00:04:42,525
A Malaysian government minister later said
that official was Najib Razak.

68
00:04:43,525 --> 00:04:49,918
Add it all up, and the US Justice
Department says more than 4.5 billion

69
00:04:49,918 --> 00:04:54,420
dollars was taken from 1MDB between 2009
and 2015.

70
00:04:54,670 --> 00:04:59,769
Malaysia's anti-corruption agency puts the
total misappropriated at around 42 billion

71
00:04:59,769 --> 00:05:03,009
ringgit, roughly ten billion dollars at
today's rates.

72
00:05:04,209 --> 00:05:05,509
So where did it go?

73
00:05:05,509 --> 00:05:10,848
According to US prosecutors, the money
paid for a lifestyle that looked like a

74
00:05:10,848 --> 00:05:11,258
movie.

75
00:05:11,258 --> 00:05:16,323
Some of it went into a Hollywood
production company, Red Granite Pictures,

76
00:05:16,323 --> 00:05:18,718
which made The Wolf of Wall Street.

77
00:05:18,968 --> 00:05:24,900
In 2018, Red Granite agreed to pay the US
government sixty million dollars to settle

78
00:05:24,900 --> 00:05:25,536
the case.

79
00:05:26,086 --> 00:05:29,822
There was art. A Picasso. A Basquiat
collage.

80
00:05:29,822 --> 00:05:36,132
According to the Justice Department, Jho
Low gave both as gifts to the actor

81
00:05:36,132 --> 00:05:37,627
Leonardo DiCaprio.

82
00:05:37,877 --> 00:05:43,094
DiCaprio was never accused of any
wrongdoing, and he handed them over to US

83
00:05:43,094 --> 00:05:48,312
authorities, along with an Oscar statuette
once won by Marlon Brando, which

84
00:05:48,312 --> 00:05:50,538
prosecutors say was also a gift.

85
00:05:51,088 --> 00:05:53,050
And there was the yacht.

86
00:05:53,050 --> 00:05:59,019
Equanimity. Three hundred feet long, with
a helipad, a pool and a cinema.

87
00:05:59,019 --> 00:06:05,642
According to the Justice Department, it
was bought for around 250 million dollars

88
00:06:05,642 --> 00:06:06,787
of 1MDB money.

89
00:06:07,787 --> 00:06:12,517
Meanwhile, back in Kuala Lumpur, the fund
was drowning in debt.

90
00:06:12,517 --> 00:06:18,373
By 2015, 1MDB owed around eleven billion
dollars and was struggling to pay its

91
00:06:18,373 --> 00:06:19,274
bondholders.

92
00:06:19,274 --> 00:06:24,605
The people who would eventually pay that
bill were Malaysian taxpayers.

93
00:06:25,805 --> 00:06:29,532
The story broke because a few people
refused to look away.

94
00:06:29,532 --> 00:06:34,673
In London, a British journalist named
Clare Rewcastle Brown ran a website called

95
00:06:34,673 --> 00:06:35,637
Sarawak Report.

96
00:06:35,637 --> 00:06:40,714
In Kuala Lumpur, the business newspaper
The Edge was asking the same questions.

97
00:06:40,964 --> 00:06:47,189
In 2015, both obtained a huge cache of
internal emails from PetroSaudi, taken by

98
00:06:47,189 --> 00:06:48,590
a former employee.

99
00:06:48,590 --> 00:06:54,192
The emails, they reported, showed how the
joint-venture money had moved.

100
00:06:54,742 --> 00:06:59,678
Then, on July 2, 2015, The Wall Street
Journal reported that Malaysian

101
00:06:59,678 --> 00:07:05,177
investigators had traced nearly 700
million dollars into Najib's personal bank

102
00:07:05,177 --> 00:07:05,812
accounts.

103
00:07:05,812 --> 00:07:11,241
Najib denied taking any money for personal
gain and blamed political enemies.

104
00:07:11,791 --> 00:07:14,488
What happened next shocked Malaysians.

105
00:07:14,488 --> 00:07:20,236
Within weeks, the attorney general who was
leading the investigation was removed.

106
00:07:20,236 --> 00:07:25,488
The deputy prime minister, who had raised
questions about 1MDB, was fired.

107
00:07:25,738 --> 00:07:29,799
Sarawak Report was blocked inside
Malaysia. The Edge's newspapers were

108
00:07:29,799 --> 00:07:31,366
suspended for three months.

109
00:07:32,366 --> 00:07:37,273
In January 2016, the new attorney general
announced his conclusion.

110
00:07:37,273 --> 00:07:42,985
The 681 million dollars, he said, was a
personal donation from the Saudi royal

111
00:07:42,985 --> 00:07:47,818
family. Most of it had been returned. No
crime had been committed.

112
00:07:47,818 --> 00:07:50,308
Case closed. At least in Malaysia.

113
00:07:51,508 --> 00:07:56,971
But the money had flowed through banks all
over the world. And other countries were

114
00:07:56,971 --> 00:07:58,353
not closing anything.

115
00:07:58,353 --> 00:08:03,553
In July 2016, the US Justice Department
filed civil lawsuits to seize more than

116
00:08:03,553 --> 00:08:05,528
one billion dollars in assets.

117
00:08:05,778 --> 00:08:10,789
It called the case the largest single
action ever brought under its Kleptocracy

118
00:08:10,789 --> 00:08:15,801
Asset Recovery Initiative, a programme
built to recover money stolen by foreign

119
00:08:15,801 --> 00:08:16,435
officials.

120
00:08:16,985 --> 00:08:23,282
Singapore's regulator shut down the local
operations of two Swiss private banks, BSI

121
00:08:23,282 --> 00:08:26,806
and Falcon, over failures linked to 1MDB
money.

122
00:08:26,806 --> 00:08:30,854
Switzerland opened criminal investigations
of its own.

123
00:08:31,404 --> 00:08:37,728
At home, Malaysians filled the streets of
Kuala Lumpur in yellow T-shirts, in mass

124
00:08:37,728 --> 00:08:43,666
rallies organized by the reform movement
Bersih, calling for Najib to resign.

125
00:08:43,666 --> 00:08:49,296
In August 2015, one of those rallies went
on for two days and two nights.

126
00:08:49,296 --> 00:08:50,144
He did not.

127
00:08:50,394 --> 00:08:53,111
His coalition still controlled parliament.

128
00:08:53,111 --> 00:08:57,380
There was only one place left where he
could lose. The ballot box.

129
00:08:58,580 --> 00:09:02,357
On May 9, 2018, Malaysia held a general
election.

130
00:09:02,357 --> 00:09:08,524
Najib's coalition and its forerunner had
ruled the country without a break since

131
00:09:08,524 --> 00:09:10,142
independence in 1957.

132
00:09:10,142 --> 00:09:10,759
It lost.

133
00:09:11,009 --> 00:09:16,490
The new prime minister was Mahathir
Mohamad, aged 92, Najib's former mentor,

134
00:09:16,490 --> 00:09:20,457
who had come out of retirement to campaign
against him.

135
00:09:21,007 --> 00:09:24,759
Days later, police raided properties
linked to Najib.

136
00:09:24,759 --> 00:09:30,775
They said they seized hundreds of designer
handbags, thousands of pieces of jewellery

137
00:09:30,775 --> 00:09:36,296
and more than 100 million ringgit in cash.
Police valued the haul at up to 1.1

138
00:09:36,296 --> 00:09:37,429
billion ringgit.

139
00:09:37,679 --> 00:09:43,610
Najib's side disputed that figure and said
many items were gifts.

140
00:09:43,610 --> 00:09:46,895
On July 3, 2018, Najib was arrested.

141
00:09:47,445 --> 00:09:52,968
And the yacht? In February 2018, at the
request of the United States, Indonesian

142
00:09:52,968 --> 00:09:55,937
authorities had seized Equanimity off
Bali.

143
00:09:56,187 --> 00:10:02,771
In August, it was handed over to Malaysia.
The next year it was sold for 126 million

144
00:10:02,771 --> 00:10:07,161
dollars, about half of what prosecutors
say it had cost.

145
00:10:08,361 --> 00:10:15,435
The first trial was about a smaller piece
of the story. SRC International, a former

146
00:10:15,435 --> 00:10:21,741
1MDB subsidiary, and 42 million ringgit
that ended up in Najib's accounts.

147
00:10:21,991 --> 00:10:27,478
In July 2020, the High Court found him
guilty on all seven charges: abuse of

148
00:10:27,478 --> 00:10:31,304
power, criminal breach of trust and money
laundering.

149
00:10:31,304 --> 00:10:32,965
Twelve years in prison.

150
00:10:33,215 --> 00:10:38,397
The appeal court upheld it. In August
2022, so did the Federal Court, Malaysia's

151
00:10:38,397 --> 00:10:41,183
highest. Najib went to prison the same
day.

152
00:10:41,183 --> 00:10:45,393
He became the first former Malaysian prime
minister to be jailed.

153
00:10:45,943 --> 00:10:51,074
In 2024, the Pardons Board cut his
sentence in half, to six years.

154
00:10:52,074 --> 00:10:59,143
Then came the big one. The main 1MDB
trial, about some 2.3 billion ringgit that

155
00:10:59,143 --> 00:11:03,348
flowed into his accounts between 2011 and
2014.

156
00:11:03,348 --> 00:11:10,775
On December 26, 2025, after a trial that
lasted more than six years, the High Court

157
00:11:10,775 --> 00:11:14,891
found Najib guilty on all twenty-five
charges.

158
00:11:15,141 --> 00:11:21,494
Fifteen years in prison, and a fine of
11.38 billion ringgit, roughly 2.8 billion

159
00:11:21,494 --> 00:11:22,121
dollars.

160
00:11:22,121 --> 00:11:27,062
The judge ruled that the Saudi donation
letters were forgeries.

161
00:11:27,062 --> 00:11:33,728
He found that Jho Low had acted as Najib's
proxy, and that Najib had not been misled.

162
00:11:33,978 --> 00:11:37,902
Jho Low was not on trial there, and he
denies wrongdoing.

163
00:11:37,902 --> 00:11:41,207
Najib is appealing, and maintains his
innocence.

164
00:11:41,757 --> 00:11:47,031
In September 2026, the King granted him a
conditional pardon in the SRC case. Once

165
00:11:47,031 --> 00:11:52,306
he pays a fifty-million-ringgit fine, he
can serve the rest of that sentence under

166
00:11:52,306 --> 00:11:53,142
house arrest.

167
00:11:53,392 --> 00:11:59,841
His fifteen-year 1MDB sentence is due to
start in August 2028. He has asked the

168
00:11:59,841 --> 00:12:02,698
court to suspend it pending appeal.

169
00:12:03,248 --> 00:12:09,049
In America, Goldman Sachs admitted its
role. In 2020 it agreed to pay more than

170
00:12:09,049 --> 00:12:14,998
2.9 billion dollars, and its Malaysian
unit pleaded guilty to bribery conspiracy.

171
00:12:14,998 --> 00:12:20,505
Former Goldman banker Roger Ng was
convicted by a jury and sentenced to ten

172
00:12:20,505 --> 00:12:20,946
years.

173
00:12:21,196 --> 00:12:26,504
His former boss, Tim Leissner, pleaded
guilty, cooperated, and got two years.

174
00:12:27,704 --> 00:12:29,170
What about the money?

175
00:12:29,170 --> 00:12:34,405
The US Justice Department says it has
returned about 1.4 billion dollars to

176
00:12:34,405 --> 00:12:35,033
Malaysia.

177
00:12:35,033 --> 00:12:40,896
In 2019, Jho Low and his family agreed to
give up assets worth more than 700 million

178
00:12:40,896 --> 00:12:45,643
dollars, including properties in Beverly
Hills, New York and London.

179
00:12:45,893 --> 00:12:51,301
He said the deal was not an admission of
guilt. The US said it did not end any

180
00:12:51,301 --> 00:12:52,480
criminal charges.

181
00:12:53,030 --> 00:12:58,195
Malaysia says it has recovered over 31
billion ringgit, including two and a half

182
00:12:58,195 --> 00:13:00,971
billion dollars in cash from Goldman
Sachs.

183
00:13:01,221 --> 00:13:08,123
But the debts came first. By 2025, more
than 42 billion ringgit had gone into

184
00:13:08,123 --> 00:13:14,487
repaying 1MDB's loans, over 15 billion of
it from the Finance Ministry.

185
00:13:14,487 --> 00:13:19,237
And repayments on the last 1MDB bonds run
until 2039.

186
00:13:19,787 --> 00:13:25,104
Malaysia's anti-corruption chief says the
country's recovery rate is among the

187
00:13:25,104 --> 00:13:30,421
highest in the world. But every ringgit
spent on 1MDB's debts was public money

188
00:13:30,421 --> 00:13:33,897
that could have built schools, hospitals
and roads.

189
00:13:33,897 --> 00:13:36,760
Jho Low, meanwhile, has never been caught.

190
00:13:37,010 --> 00:13:41,734
Malaysia and the United States want him.
His whereabouts have never been officially

191
00:13:41,734 --> 00:13:42,303
confirmed.

192
00:13:43,503 --> 00:13:45,558
So what does 1MDB teach us?

193
00:13:45,558 --> 00:13:51,496
One. When the person who approves the
spending is also the person in charge of

194
00:13:51,496 --> 00:13:53,932
the checks, there are no checks.

195
00:13:54,182 --> 00:14:00,174
Two. A fund that raises billions with no
clear business, and pays its bankers far

196
00:14:00,174 --> 00:14:03,503
above market rates, is telling you
something.

197
00:14:03,503 --> 00:14:09,347
Three. Watch the names. A company that
sounds like a real partner is not always

198
00:14:09,347 --> 00:14:10,605
the real partner.

199
00:14:10,855 --> 00:14:16,127
Four. When an audit is classified secret,
an attorney general is replaced and

200
00:14:16,127 --> 00:14:19,687
newspapers are suspended, that is not a
coincidence.

201
00:14:19,687 --> 00:14:22,563
And five. Follow the money across borders.

202
00:14:22,813 --> 00:14:28,164
In the end, this case was not cracked
first inside Malaysia. It was cracked by

203
00:14:28,164 --> 00:14:33,651
banks, regulators and prosecutors in other
countries, and by a few reporters who

204
00:14:33,651 --> 00:14:34,749
refused to stop.

205
00:14:35,949 --> 00:14:40,808
Equanimity was sold to a Malaysian casino
operator and given a new name:

206
00:14:40,808 --> 00:14:41,617
Tranquility.

207
00:14:41,617 --> 00:14:45,531
The film is still watched. The bonds are
still being paid.

208
00:14:45,531 --> 00:14:50,322
And for many Malaysians, the question is
not only where the money went.

209
00:14:50,322 --> 00:14:53,089
It is how long everyone allowed it to go.

210
00:14:53,339 --> 00:14:58,405
And the man US prosecutors place at the
center of it all has still never faced a

211
00:14:58,405 --> 00:14:59,038
courtroom.

212
00:14:59,588 --> 00:15:01,450
Next time: another case file.

